r pattern recognition  use regular expressions such an  innocuous sounding word has anyone ever  used regular expressions here how many  people love them how many people hate  them how many people are like it depends  on the task yeah okay so this is usually  hit or miss in this case it worked  beautifully and brilliantly and I'll  show you an example the R package to use  is string r do not try to use base R for  this I did the whole thing in base are I  think a man are purest and I'm going to  use only base R for this and bad idea  bad so string are makes this so much  easier thank you to the tiny verse so  here's an example let's see if text that  is the project's AR - L 1281 and AR - LD  1302 were approved in 2018 our task we  want to search for the project numbers  so picture this is one big huge long 50  page PDF document and I just want to  pick out those particular citations  right and so here's the regular  expression you can have a look they're  not that hard to pick up the simpler the  better if they start to get really  complicated then maybe you want to try  something not a regular something other  than a regular expression but there's  lots of tutorials online and I think  they start to become kind of like a fun  word puzzle so you can have you have the  text like this and then you just use the  string our function which is string  extract all and then voila you get the  numbers out so imagine having a 50 page  document and that's how easy it is to  get these little citations so review why  you string are packaged well here's a  fun crossword for you and all of the  answers are string R so for example for  example it is built it is part of the  tidy verse universe it has its own  handy-dandy cheat sheet from our studio  which you can just google and find  it's much better than besar all kinds of  reasons so remember to use string r for  regular expressions so here's my  specific example from the inter-american  Development Bank the mission is to  reduc