ability of sighting each  other than a loan that's Argentina and  Brazil so you would model this  statistically by asking what is the  probability of a link between two  arbitrary look notes the outcome is one  there's a link or zero there's no link  what kind of models this remind you of  I'm sorry like a logistic regression  right except there is interdependence  across the nodes so that messes up the  logistic regression and then you know  that it's like a really nasty time  series or geospatial model where you  have to model all this interdependence  across all the units and to avoid that  whole problem you do mark Monte Carlo  Markov chain and this model does just  that it's called exponential random  graph model it was developed by the stat  net team at the University of Washington  and they're always giving workshops  about this all over the world basically  so there's a lot of good literature on  this model and the package at R is  called ogum so here are the results  sorry to show you numbers basically you  can't include too many covariance  because the the MCMC doesn't like too  many so it's very delicate and it takes  a long time to run but just focus for a  second on the final two so we can see  that with if two nodes have the same  country then there's a positive effect  on the probability of a link and it's  significant in the same way if two  countries have the same division or the  same economic sector then again there's  a positive impact on the probability of  a link but you can see the the country  the shared country effect is stronger  than the shared division effect so you  interpret it in the same way as a  logistic regression you can calculate  the probabilities like so and then you  can compare the probabilities and it  turns out it's four point around four  four times more likely that countries  with the same that projects were the  same country share a link than project  with the same division why  this be interesting to an organization  li